  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A large upper level ridge of high\npressure continues to situate over the southwestern CONUS, keeping\nhigh temperatures 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of\nyear. The higher temperatures reaching into the 100s have prompted\nseveral Heat Warnings and Advisories over central and northern\nNevada. Extreme Heat Warning is in effect today until Sunday\nafternoon for eastern Elko County from Wells to West Wendover.\nHeat Advisory is in effect for Ruby Mountains, northern Elko\nCounty and White Pine County until Sunday afternoon. Rest of\ncentral and northern Nevada are under a Heat Advisory today and\nwill be under Extreme Heat Warning starting 6 AM Friday. For \nHumboldt County, the Extreme Heat Warning will last until 11 PM \nFriday, for southwestern Elko, Lander, Eureka, and N. Nye County, \nthe Extreme Heat Warning will last until 11 PM Saturday.\n\nA tighter weather gradient just to the north of Nevada will\nincrease westerly wind speeds across the northern zones along the\nstate border. Elevated fire weather conditions for those zones due\nto afternoon breezes, low relative humidity, and extreme heat.\n\nThe position of the upper ridge is aiding in producing a monsoonal\nflow over the southwest with Arizona and the Baja being the main\nareas for moisture. The ridge will push some of that moisture into\nsouthern Nevada and reaching into central Nevada by Friday\nafternoon, with PWAT values increasing up to 0.5-0.7 inches across\nN. Nye and White Pine Counties. Despite the increase in moisture,\nthunderstorm development is expected to be at mid-high levels in\nthe atmosphere, prompting the storms to be more dry than wet,\nhowever at this time, probabilities of thunderstorm development\nremains low for Friday. Over the weekend, probabilities increase\nfor dry thunderstorm development across central Nevada. Storm\nmotions will be slow which may prompt thunderstorms to develop and\ncollapse on themselves as storm downdrafts will subdue the \nupdrafts. As a result, the storms may be short lived and any \noutflow wind gusts may reach up to 50 mph.\n\nActive monsoonal pattern is expected to last through mid week with\nchances for dry thunderstorms spreading towards northeastern\nNevada by Monday.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continue for\nnear record heat across northern and central Nevada for the\nremainder of the week and weekend. Moderate confidence of elevated\nfire weather conditions across the northern zones Friday and\nSaturday. Low confidence for dry thunderstorm activity across\ncentral Nevada Friday and Saturday afternoons. Changes to the\nweather grids for Friday to better reflect dry thunderstorm\npotential.",
